// === PREAMBLE ===
// @trope    Villain Protagonist
// @category Character Roles / Villains
// @source   https://allthetropes.org/wiki/Villain_Protagonist
// @version  1.3
// @domain    Narrative
//
// Laconic (per the wiki): the bad guy IS the star — the central figure the story is about is a genuine
// Villain, not merely a rough Anti-Hero. The discriminator is the conjunction on ONE character: the
// focus of the story ([+Central], whose choice drives the central arc) AND the commission of real
// villainy. Distinct from the Anti-Hero (sympathetic, unscrupulous, but not the antagonal evil) and
// from a story whose villain is merely a side character: here the protagonist and the villain are the
// same person. Builds on `the_protagonist`.


// === IMPORTS & ASSOCIATIONS ===
import "trl/concepts/index.trl"            // conflict

assoc villain_protagonist -> [
  the_protagonist : "the villainous form of",
  the_big_bad     : "is the dark pole AND the focus at once, unlike a",
  conflict        : "drives, from the wrong side of, a"
]


// === ONTOLOGY ===

// — founded here (the villainous nature is the trope's discriminator; promotion model, cf. the_wizard) —
prop Villainous [+Narrative]    // genuinely the bad guy — the antagonal evil, not a rough Anti-Hero

imply VillainProtagonist -> [Villainous, Protagonist, Narrative]


// === ABSTRACT RULE ===
// Naming rule: the story's CENTRAL figure who DRIVES the central arc and yet commits genuine villainy
// is the Villain Protagonist. Two events bind $p: the agency that makes them the protagonist
// ([&Initiates], thread=$story) and the villainy that makes them the villain ([&Betrays], target=the
// one wronged). Both name $p as agent, so phase-2 coverage re-checks [+Central] [+Villainous] on the
// one bound character (STYLE §8). The positive discriminator is [+Villainous] + a shown betrayal, not
// the mere absence of heroism — and not the [+Unscrupulous] roughness of an Anti-Hero.
rule Villain_Protagonist {
  when:
    arc  $story
    char $p [+Central] [+Villainous]
    evt  $turn [&Initiates(agent=$p, thread=$story)]
    evt  $deed [&Betrays(agent=$p, target=$victim)]
  then:
    $p [+Protagonist]
    $p [+VillainProtagonist]
    $p -- $story
    $p -- conflict
}


// === CONCRETE VIGNETTE: Macbeth — the Thane who would be king (stage play, Jacobean) ===
// The play is unmistakably ABOUT Macbeth — his ambition is the engine of every act. And what that
// ambition drives him to is no roguish misdeed but the blackest villainy: he murders Duncan, his king
// and his guest, asleep under his own roof, and climbs the corpse to the throne. The protagonist and
// the villain are one man.

// MACBETH — the central figure; the story is his, and so is the crime
char macbeth [+Central] [+Villainous]

// KING DUNCAN — his king and his guest, murdered in his sleep
char duncan [+Sovereign]

// THE RISE TO THE THRONE — the central arc Macbeth's choice sets in motion
arc the_usurpation

scene dunsinane {
  beat 1 {
    macbeth -- the_usurpation
    // ambition sets the whole tragedy in motion — the protagonist's driving choice
    evt the_resolve [&Initiates(agent=macbeth, thread=the_usurpation)]   // "I am settled" — he commits to the deed
  }
  beat 2 {
    // the villainy itself: he betrays king and host at once, murdering Duncan in his sleep
    evt the_murder [&Betrays(agent=macbeth, target=duncan)]
    // Villain_Protagonist derives: macbeth [+Protagonist] [+VillainProtagonist] -- the_usurpation -- conflict
  }
}
